.Dd June 5, 2026
.Dt CAP_RT_CHANNEL 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cap_rt_channel
.Nd CAP_RT bidirectional capability channel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load at boot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
echo 'cap_rt_channel_load="YES"' >> /boot/loader.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Wire protocol header:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <dev/cap_rt/cap_rt_channel_proto.h>
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
service creates connected channels of capability descriptors for
peer-to-peer messaging.
Each channel consists of two endpoints; a message sent on one endpoint
is delivered to the other.
.Pp
The service is registered under the name
.Dq channel
with the
.Dv CAP_RT_SVC_MINTABLE
flag, allowing holders to mint additional instances via
.Dv CAP_RT_MINT_INSTANCE .
.Ss Protocol
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Connect to
.Dq channel
via
.Dv CAP_RT_CONNECT
to obtain endpoint A.
.It
Send
.Dv CHANNEL_OP_CREATE
via
.Dv CAP_RT_SENDMSG
on A.
.It
Receive the reply via
.Dv CAP_RT_RECVMSG ;
the reply carries endpoint B as an attached fd.
.It
Endpoints A and B are now connected:
.Dv CAP_RT_SENDMSG
on A delivers to
.Dv CAP_RT_RECVMSG
on B, and vice versa.
.It
Closing either endpoint revokes the other.
.El
.Ss Wire Constants
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#define CHANNEL_OP_CREATE  1
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Dv CHANNEL_OP_CREATE
operation is the only operation.
It is sent as a
.Vt uint32_t
payload via
.Dv CAP_RT_SENDMSG
on an unconnected instance.
The reply, received via
.Dv CAP_RT_RECVMSG ,
carries the peer fd.
.Ss Message Forwarding
After connecting, messages are forwarded to the peer via the kernel's
.Fn cap_rt_forward
path.
The original sender's credentials (badge, token, and credential trailer)
are preserved through the forward.
Forwarding is fire-and-forget; the sender receives no reply.
.Ss Revocation
Closing or revoking one endpoint delivers
.Er ECONNRESET
to the peer.
Any subsequent
.Dv CAP_RT_SENDMSG
on the surviving endpoint returns
.Er ECONNRESET .
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The following DTrace probes are provided under the
.Nm cap_rt_channel
provider:
.Bl -tag -width "handler-done"
.It Cm create Ns Pq Ar badge_a, badge_b, error
Fires when a channel is successfully created.
.Ar badge_a
and
.Ar badge_b
are the badges of the two endpoints.
.It Cm forward Ns Pq Ar src_badge, dst_badge, datalen
Fires when a message is forwarded from one endpoint to its peer.
.It Cm handler-done Ns Pq Ar op, badge, error, elapsed
Fires on completion of every handler invocation.
.Ar elapsed
is an
.Vt sbintime_t
duration.
.It Cm state Ns Pq Ar event, op, badge, error, aux
Fires on state changes including
.Dq mint-error ,
.Dq peer-dead ,
and
.Dq revoke .
.El
.Pp
Example one-liner to trace channel creation:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -n 'cap_rt_channel::create { printf("a=%u b=%u err=%d",
    arg0, arg1, arg2); }'
.Ed
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a channel and exchange a message:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <dev/cap_rt/cap_rt_channel_proto.h>

int a_fd, b_fd;
uint32_t op;

/* Connect to the channel service */
a_fd = cap_rt_connect("channel");

/* Create the channel */
op = CHANNEL_OP_CREATE;
cap_rt_sendmsg(a_fd, &op, sizeof(op), NULL, 0);

/* Receive the peer fd */
b_fd = cap_rt_recvmsg(a_fd, NULL, 0);

/* Now a_fd and b_fd are connected */
cap_rt_sendmsg(a_fd, msg, msglen, NULL, 0);
cap_rt_recvmsg(b_fd, buf, buflen);  /* receives msg */
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ECONNRESET
The peer endpoint has been closed or revoked.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The message payload is too small, or an operation other than
.Dv CHANNEL_OP_CREATE
was sent on an unconnected instance.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_rt 4 ,
.Xr cap_rt_identity 4 ,
.Xr oracled 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in 5BSD.
